        "section": [
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">COPD exacerbation, treated with bronchodilators and steroids</div>"
            },
            "code": {
              "coding": [
                {
                  "code": "67781-5",
                  "system": "http://loinc.org",
                  "display": "Summarization of encounter note"
                }
              ]
            },
            "title": "Synthesis"
          },
          {
            "text": {
              "status": "generated",
              "div": "<div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\">Dear Colleague, I am writing to inform you about the recent hospitalisation of your patient, a 67-year-old male, admitted on 08/10/1996 with an acute exacerbation of his known chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. On admission, he presented with increased dyspnoea, productive cough, and reduced oxygen saturation. Chest X-ray confirmed hyperinflation without consolidation. Arterial blood gas analysis revealed mild hypoxaemia with hypercapnia. Blood tests showed moderate inflammatory markers. He was treated with nebulised bronchodilators, systemic corticosteroids, supplemental oxygen therapy, and supportive intravenous fluids. Clinical response was satisfactory, with improvement in oxygenation and symptom relief. The patient received education regarding inhaler technique and exacerbation management prior to discharge. Please arrange early follow-up and consider pulmonary rehabilitation referral. Discharge medications include inhaled bronchodilators and a tapering course of oral steroids. Yours sincerely, Dr. [Hospital Physician]</div>"
            },
